NAME
    ls — List directory contents.

SYNOPSIS
    ls [OPTIONS] [PATH]

DESCRIPTION
    The 'ls' command lists the contents of a directory. By default, it lists
    files and directories in the current directory.

    Hidden files are not shown by default, because apparently some files
    prefer to remain mysterious.

USAGE
    > ls            # Lists the contents of the current directory.
    > ls <path>     # Lists the contents of the specified directory.
    > ls -a         # Includes hidden files and directories.
    > ls -l         # Shows detailed information about each entry.
    > ls -a -l      # Includes hidden files and detailed information.

FUN FACTS
    - Directories are displayed with a "/" so you know they're directories.
    - Hidden files aren't actually invisible. They're just hiding.
    - "ls -a" is how you discover files you didn't know were there.
    - "ls -l" makes looking at files feel significantly more professional.

EXAMPLES
    > ls
        Lists the files and directories in the current directory.

    > ls documents
        Lists the contents of the "documents" directory.

    > ls -a
        Lists everything, including hidden files.

    > ls -l
        Lists files with permissions, size, modification time, and name.

    > ls -a -l documents
        Lists all files in "documents" with detailed information.

SEE ALSO
    help(1), cd(1), man(1)

    Remember, 'ls' is just your CLI asking:
    "What's in here?"

